  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "1fac6160-def1-4726-89a3-42d23d64d899",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "what are flops ?? ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "e61519a2-face-401f-81d6-fc7b5f8612f4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "you see they are been used every where and kinda know that they determine the speed of any system better flops mean better machine that is half true as there are many other things that work together to get the high throughput of a machine in the form of output like imagine if a you have v8 engine but car chaise is made up of cardboard then it would not  hold up in that speed and fall through this is analogous to high flops and low bandwidth this blogs aims to get through certain terminologies that are someone would bump into while go through techincal  papers and blogs ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "696a2533-a50e-4d9e-9256-ef528f58a777",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "4c76c8b6-4c49-41b2-b361-869c270c1c70",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "so what are flops ?",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "d4420ef3-f641-480e-ad05-b9724342b30a",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "any floating point operation is considered as one flop ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "9bd999e7-3e12-40db-8478-61acb71ba381",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "(a+b)/c  would required two floating point operation thats two flops to do it ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "eeb7e80e-49b3-48b1-9b47-40a4909b730e",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "so yeh number of floating point operation = = number of flops",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "8a54dc2e-7a40-4f8f-880a-cd18bce00370",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "so just to be clear flop is a number like 200 jules of energy required its like total work needed to be done to complete that task ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "bc8bfcde-d73d-4eb8-b6d4-b0c73b284f7a",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "and flops/s is the way to tell how fast a machine can perform that task and H100 can do 990TFLOPS/s  ( 990 trillion floating point  operation in one sec)  there is a percision of that floating point that effects different things but we will talk about that in a while ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ab0ed819-a650-49db-b40d-4fce3eab7dd2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "so just remember when we talk about model we usually say we it to so and so flops but when we talk about the chip we talk about flops/s ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "2c19f9b9-d699-433b-a8fb-174a3ee1169a",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "cdcc3bdd-f915-43ca-a1a0-7f0533953d72",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "ok lets then calculate the flops of training run ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "a3ae2dca-0ddf-4531-acf6-91adb624a45a",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "first lets see flops required to multiple matrix and build up from there ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "03651089-08a1-4d48-b5bc-4a2d0bff7044",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " M(m*k dim) and N (k*n dim)  ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "c8e70274-dc6c-4887-9117-e2b879d707b2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now you get the idea of flops are just any arethimatic operation ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "55042d40-c567-4e5b-b195-3fe4d13cdd13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "for  a matrix mul the number of flops are  m . n  2(k-1) we drop the -ve one as it is negligble ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "5a39503e-e28d-4e93-bea4-8217841d6706",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "each multiplication and addition are added to total flops but what if most of our matrix is made of zero or one ?? as humans we know these cases are special mul cases hence we dont need to calculate them atleast so do they get added in our total flops counts ----- yes :(",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "d568b580-d23d-4ae5-b760-0e170d2db7a8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "now I believe that you might have heard about kernal engineering where they make customs mat mul to optimizes these operation now you might get the idea about 1's and 0's that we talked abt yeah that is not kernal engineering rather they never reduces ( making customs kernal ) the total number of flops ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ca2c1b07-6cff-4f01-afb2-7396442e4ed1",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "4d211db6-f565-4dd7-97d4-e9fc65ac2310",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Cores vs threads ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "07075eac-40b2-420a-bf36-e1c40885a3c6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "7a49df41-c34c-42a4-8dab-9a1eee4fbe29",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "physical core  - that is something on the cpu itself and logical cores are basically the threads of the computer ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "c1d9c5f7-9277-4333-984d-017c0d0a3217",
      "type": "image",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "name": "image.png",
        "url": "/uploads/1788190178887-image.png",
        "caption": "",
        "showPreview": true
      },
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "0322eae3-51da-4f6f-b157-1a1562030546",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4 cores 8 threads",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "b2189290-ac72-4195-95ce-fccc3185792c",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "now all the four cores can work simultaneously but each can only work on one thread at a time so 4 threads simultaneously in this case ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "24a6f2d4-7ad7-42f6-9d9b-90d9b4b9f35e",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "cores themselves cannot work on multiple threads",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "70513a36-06c4-490c-ba03-a3a6dcda83fc",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "switching btw this threads is what we call concurrent execution and the cores operate in Parelle  concurrency vs Parellisum concurrency gives you the illusion of parllisum   ",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "f29c1968-ecbb-461d-9523-38e43f6e4bd5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "textColor": "default",
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "just so you know each core has like multiple adders and multipliers and stuff so each thread can get executed like simultaneously as at one time thread a would require a multiplier and b would need a adder hence when they dont need the same resource they can share them but there are times they have to wait up for a thread to free up a particular resource \n",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
